# Comments are required

A production file under `frontend/src/` or `backend/src/` is incomplete without comments.
Do **not** ship a new or edited module that is only code. Do **not** defer comments
to a later change. “The code is self-explanatory” is not an exception.

## File

Every production file starts with a **file-level JSDoc**: what the module is, who uses
it, and the one thing it owns. One or two sentences. Not a changelog.

New files must include this header **in the same change** as the file is created.

## Functions and constants

- **Every function** (exported or not) has JSDoc: purpose, params, returns, throws.
  Write it so an agent can use the function without reading the body.
- **Every exported constant** has a one-line JSDoc or `/** */` saying why the value exists.
- Do **not** use JSDoc as a type system (`@param {Type}`) unless another rule requires it.

## Schema fields

Every Mongoose schema field (and nested subdoc field) has a `//` comment on the line
above: what it means in product language, with a concrete example when it helps.
Match `backend/src/models/job.model.js`.

A new or edited model without per-field comments is not done.

```js
// ❌ BAD
userId: { type: ObjectId, ref: 'User', required: true },

// ✅ GOOD
// Inbox owner (Alice). One row per user per event.
userId: { type: ObjectId, ref: 'User', required: true },
```

## Why vs what

Comments explain **why / intent**. Do not narrate the next line (“increment i”).
A field comment that only repeats the property name is not enough.

## Tests

`backend/tests/` and `frontend/tests/` need a file-level purpose comment and JSDoc
on non-obvious helpers. They do not need a comment on every assertion.
